WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.731
It is nine in the morning on press twelve.

00:00:02.731 --> 00:00:05.669
Flash has kept returning
since the start of the shift.

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:08.000
The setter opens VP Expert at the machine.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.463
There is no need for a machine connection.

00:00:10.463 --> 00:00:12.420
Start with what is visible on the part.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.055
The plastics library
includes twenty-four defects.

00:00:16.055 --> 00:00:20.063
Here the setter selects flash: excess
material along the parting line.

00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.238
Selecting the symptom does
not identify its cause.

00:00:23.948 --> 00:00:25.438
Where on the part does it appear?

00:00:25.808 --> 00:00:28.424
When did it begin, and
how often does it happen?

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:32.326
If the operator does not know,
they can leave the answer open.

00:00:32.326 --> 00:00:33.913
An unknown is not evidence.

00:00:34.623 --> 00:00:38.466
Start with the proposed check. The
reference case examines how the mould

00:00:38.466 --> 00:00:40.843
closes before moving
further down the list.

00:00:41.213 --> 00:00:43.960
Open the explanation to see
why this check is relevant.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.544
Work through the checks rather than
jumping straight to a setting change.

00:00:48.254 --> 00:00:52.090
Record a check as conforming,
nonconforming, or not verified.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:55.224
A nonconforming finding is
a lead, not a diagnosis.

00:00:55.594 --> 00:00:58.347
If the result is
inconclusive, record that too.

00:00:58.347 --> 00:01:01.508
The next person needs to know
what has not been ruled out.

00:01:02.218 --> 00:01:05.823
After an authorised trial, record
the cause supported by the checks,

00:01:05.823 --> 00:01:07.601
the action taken and the outcome.

00:01:07.971 --> 00:01:10.280
In this example the defect disappears.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.951
A favourable result supports the hypothesis;
quality still has to validate it.

00:01:15.661 --> 00:01:19.586
The summary brings the observations
and the result together for review.

00:01:19.586 --> 00:01:23.041
A validated case can be recalled
when the same defect returns.

00:01:23.411 --> 00:01:27.071
The example is illustrative. Your
workshop builds its own memory from

00:01:27.071 --> 00:01:30.080
reviewed cases, including
attempts that did not work.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:35.583
VP Expert starts with the defect an
operator sees. On connectable presses,

00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:38.482
VP Predict can also flag
a measurement drift.

00:01:38.482 --> 00:01:41.086
The team stays in control in both cases.
